<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n110528" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">φέναξ</span></b> , <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ᾱκος</span>, ὁ, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>cheat, quack, impostor,</i>
			<i><span class="en">
				Ar.
				<u>Ra.</u>
				909
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Heraclit.
				<u>Ep.</u>
				6.3
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Porph.
				<u>Chr.</u>
				29
			</span></i>, etc.; in <i><span class="en">
				Ar.
				<u>Ach.</u>
				89
			</span></i>, perhaps with a play on <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">φοῖνιξ</span> (the bird); in <i><span class="en">
				<u>Eq.</u>
				634
			</span></i>
			<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Φένακες</span> are addressed as the
			tutelary gods of cheats.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
